WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:21.800
"Just take a few deep breaths and try to relax," Ray Aguilera said to his friend Parker Brantley.

2
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:29.700
"Are you sure I should try out for a part in the play?" Parker jiggled his leg as they sat in the auditorium.

3
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:51.520
The two eighth-graders were waiting to audition for Annie , the middle-school musical. Parker watched anxiously as more kids entered the auditorium. At least five girls were practicing "Tomorrow," the only Annie song he'd ever heard. A guy came dancing down the aisle and high-fived Ray.

4
00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:02.420
"Excellent moves, Ben!" Ray said as the dancing guy passed. Ben wiggled his hands in response.

5
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.940
"Nice jazz hands!" Ray called out. "Ben's our best dancer," he said to Parker.

6
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:27.220
"I've never been in a real show before, never mind a musical." Parker cleared his throat. "The last time I was in a play, I was an apple. The performance was held in our second-grade classroom. We didn't even have a stage."

7
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:35.040
"The dental health play!" Ray grinned. "I had a huge role. I played the dentist!"

8
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.820
Parker laughed. "Yeah, you were a star even back then."

9
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.120
A girl with long, dark hair waved at Ray from across the auditorium.

10
00:01:48.300 --> 00:02:08.060
"Hey, Lakshmi!" he called out. "She played my girlfriend last year in Fiddler on the Roof ," Ray explained to Parker. "Look, you'll be fine. Don't worry about all these people showing off. Besides, what else are you going to do with your free time? You can't play soccer right now."

11
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:18.940
"That's for sure." Because of a knee injury, Parker was out for the season. On a whim he'd decided to join Ray at the audition for the musical.

12
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:28.559
"I know you're having second thoughts," Ray said. "But you're not trying out for a leading role. You'll probably end up in the ensemble."

13
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.440
"The ensemble? What does that mean?"

14
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:46.140
"The ensemble includes all the actors who don't have speaking roles. But don't worry—there's plenty for them to do. They're usually in some big production numbers. You know, singing and dancing."

15
00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:59.100
"Dancing?" Parker's voice squeaked. "You said all I'd have to do is sing. That'll be hard enough. You never mentioned dancing. Plus, I have this knee injury."

16
00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:12.900
"Don't worry," Ray said. "Since you're not very good, they'll stick you in the back." Ray hoped Parker could manage the dancing. He had thought it might cheer Parker up to have an after-school activity.

17
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:20.280
Ray went on. "I'm not much of a dancer either. But that might work to my advantage this time."

18
00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.260
Parker looked puzzled. "What do you mean?"

19
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:49.679
"Ms. Kwan—she's the director—she usually gives the leads to the eighth-grade kids. I've been in every show since we started middle school, so I've got a good shot at the lead. In this show the male lead is Oliver Warbucks, the billionaire guy who wants to adopt Annie. The way I see it, my only rival is Ben. But he's such a good dancer."

20
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:58.160
Parker squinted at him. "I'm not following you at all. This play stuff is totally confusing."

21
00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:17.000
"There's another male lead, a con man named Rooster," Ray explained patiently. "He has a big dance number. So I figure Ben will get Rooster, and I'll get Warbucks." Ray laughed when he saw that Parker was still shaking his head.

22
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:52.800
"You mean you've never seen Annie ? Okay . . . " Ray swung his legs over the seat in front of him. "Here’s the story. It's the 1930s in New York City. Annie lives in an orphanage with a bunch of other girls. Miss Hannigan—a really mean lady—runs the orphanage, and she makes the girls work hard all the time. Scrubbing floors, stuff like that. Annie's sure she's not an orphan—she thinks her parents are still alive."

23
00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.300
"Why does she think that?" Parker asked.

24
00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:10.000
"Her parents dropped her off on the orphanage steps when she was a baby. They left her a letter and half a locket. They said they'd keep the other half until they returned for her."

25
00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.840
"Oh, I get it! The locket gives her a way to identify them."

26
00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:42.620
"Right. Annie's 11 years old now, and she's tired of waiting for her parents to show up. So she runs away to look for them. A policeman catches her and takes her back to the orphanage. But, lucky for her, Warbucks wants an orphan to spend Christmas at his mansion. His secretary—her name's Grace—chooses Annie. Warbucks soon decides to adopt Annie."

27
00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.940
"And that's the end of the show?"

28
00:05:45.260 --> 00:06:02.519
"No way! Annie likes Warbucks, but she doesn't want him to adopt her. She wants to find her parents. She and Warbucks go on a radio show. Warbucks announces he'll give fifty thousand dollars to the couple who can prove they're Annie's parents."

29
00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.380
"Is that when her parents finally come back?"

30
00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:27.120
"No. They're actually dead. But Rooster—he's Miss Hannigan's brother—and his girlfriend pretend to be Annie's parents. Miss Hannigan tells them about the locket, so it looks like they're the real thing. But they're not. They get arrested and Warbucks adopts Annie. The end."

31
00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:32.420
"Phew," Parker said. "The plot's pretty complicated."

32
00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:45.100
Ray scratched his head. "You know, there's only one problem with playing Warbucks. He's bald, and I’m not sure I want to shave my head."

33
00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:59.860
"That would definitely be a different look for you!" Parker laughed. "Your hair's really long right now." He squinted across the auditorium. "Hey, isn't that your little sister over there?"

34
00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:16.260
Ray turned around and spotted his sister, Kyla, with some other sixth-grade girls. Kyla caught sight of Ray and waved. He ignored her and turned back to Parker. "Don't pay any attention to her."

35
00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:21.560
"That'll be pretty hard because she's coming over here with three friends."

36
00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.480
Ray put his feet on the floor and sank into his seat.

37
00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:34.620
"Hi, Ray! Hi, Parker!" Kyla beamed at her brother's friend. "What are you doing here, Parker?"

38
00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:41.660
Ray gave his sister an impatient look. "Kyla, what's my first rule of middle school?"

39
00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:48.440
"Don't talk to you or your friends." Kyla rolled her eyes, and her friends giggled.

40
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:56.060
"Your brother's cute," the shortest friend whispered. Ray heard her and turned bright red.

41
00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.660
Kyla repeated her question to Parker. "What are you doing here?"

42
00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.820
"I'm trying out."

43
00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:06.360
"So am I!" she exclaimed.

44
00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:18.600
Ray jumped to his feet. "Oh no, you're not. You know my second rule: You're not allowed to join any of my activities. And the musical is mine!"

45
00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:32.980
Kyla crossed her arms defiantly. "That's not what Mom says. Too bad, Ray. I'm trying out!" She signaled her friends to follow her. "Come on—let's go practice 'Tomorrow.'"
